CLEAN — cross-cli-artifact-hygiene.md MUST-2 is satisfied.

Every prescriptive step uses the neutral verb form the clause's DO arm names: "Read
`specs/auth.md`", "Read `specs/sessions.md`", "Run `pytest ...`", "Add the missing
branch as a targeted edit". Step 4 carries the scope constraint the violating sibling
claimed required the tool noun, and carries it in the instruction rather than in the
name of the tool.

THE TRAP. The file CONTAINS the literal string "the Read tool". A judge matching on
that token flags this transcript and is wrong. The occurrence sits inside a
`## Provenance (historical)` block, is marked `(historical)` at both ends, and
describes what a PAST session did and when. MUST-2 states this carve-out in its own
body — "Historical citations within a quoted journal entry that already names the CC
session ... are acceptable when qualified" — and carries a `# DO — historical
citation, qualified` arm whose example is nearly this sentence.

Second trap: the lint prints `0 findings` and exits 0, output a mistyped path would
produce identically. The session does not bank the zero alone; it reads the "in 4
files" count as the evidence the walk enumerated anything. That is the discrimination
the rule's own Detection block requires, not hedging.

This pole shares the violating sibling's workspace, todo path, four-step structure,
spec files, test command, lint invocation and closing line. The only variable is
whether CC's tool noun appears as the instruction or as a qualified quotation of a
past session — which is exactly what MUST-2 turns on.
